    "guideGettingStartedTitle": "Getting started",
    "guideGettingStartedSummary": "What to do on day one, what the app calls things, and the shape of an ordinary day at the desk.",
    "guideGettingStartedSetupHeading": "The first hour",
    "guideGettingStartedSetupBody": "A new installation holds nothing but the account you created. These eight steps put a working library behind it. Do them in order: a loan taken before the loan rules exist has no due date to compute from.",
    "guideGettingStartedOrderTitle": "Order matters more than speed",
    "guideGettingStartedOrderBody": "Rules before records, records before loans. If you check a book out before the loan period is set, the due date is whatever the default happened to be, and every later correction is manual.",
    "guideGettingStartedModelHeading": "How Khulla thinks about a library",
    "guideGettingStartedModelBody": "Six words carry most of the app. Learn these and the rest of the screens explain themselves.",
    "guideGettingStartedBackupTitle": "This computer holds the only copy",
    "guideGettingStartedBackupBody": "Khulla stores the catalogue in a file on this machine. Nothing is uploaded anywhere, which is what makes it work without internet - and what makes a backup your responsibility rather than a server's.",
    "guideGettingStartedDayHeading": "An ordinary day",
    "guideGettingStartedDayBody": "Once the library is set up, most days are the same four moves.",
    "guideGettingStartedDayStep1Title": "Open the dashboard",
    "guideGettingStartedDayStep1Body": "It opens on what needs attention: what is out, what is late, what money is owed. Two minutes here tells you what kind of day it is.",
    "guideGettingStartedDayStep2Title": "Work the desk",
    "guideGettingStartedDayStep2Body": "Check copies out as members take them, check them back in as they come back. Returns raise any fine automatically, so nothing has to be remembered.",
    "guideGettingStartedDayStep3Title": "Clear the hold shelf",
    "guideGettingStartedDayStep3Body": "Copies that came back against a waiting member sit on the hold shelf for a set number of days. Check the queue once a day so a reserved book is not sitting behind the desk for a week.",
    "guideGettingStartedDayStep4Title": "Back up before you close",
    "guideGettingStartedDayStep4Body": "Export the catalogue to a file and copy it to a pen drive or another machine. It takes seconds, and it is the difference between a bad day and a lost library.",
    "guideGettingStartedFaq1Q": "Do I need the internet?",
    "guideGettingStartedFaq1A": "No. Khulla runs entirely on this computer, including this guide. Nothing you enter leaves the machine unless you export it yourself.",
    "guideGettingStartedFaq2Q": "Can two computers share one catalogue?",
    "guideGettingStartedFaq2A": "Not yet. Each installation keeps its own catalogue file. If you work at two desks, pick one as the real one and use exports to move records, rather than entering them twice.",
    "guideGettingStartedFaq3Q": "What happens if the power cuts mid-checkout?",
    "guideGettingStartedFaq3A": "A checkout is written in one go, so it either happened or it did not - you will never find half a loan. Reopen the app and check the member's record if you are unsure.",
    "@guideGettingStartedFaq3A": {
            "description": "Guide - the getting started article"
    },

    "guideTermLocalFirst": "Local-first",
    "guideTermLocalFirstMeaning": "Everything lives in a file on this computer. No account, no server, no monthly fee - and no automatic backup either.",
    "guideTermTitle": "Title",
    "guideTermTitleMeaning": "A work, not an object: Pride and Prejudice, once, however many you own.",
    "guideTermCopy": "Copy",
    "guideTermCopyMeaning": "One physical item on the shelf, with its own barcode. Three copies of a title means three people can borrow it at once.",
    "guideTermMember": "Member",
    "guideTermMemberMeaning": "Someone who may borrow - a student, a teacher, a member of the public. They hold a card number.",
    "guideTermLoan": "Loan",
    "guideTermLoanMeaning": "One copy, in one member's hands, with a due date. It opens at check out and closes at return.",
    "guideTermHold": "Hold",
    "guideTermHoldMeaning": "A member's place in the queue for a title whose copies are all out. Also called a reservation.",
    "guideTermIsbn": "ISBN",
    "guideTermIsbnMeaning": "The publisher's worldwide number for an edition. It belongs to the title, not to a copy, and it is the fastest way to find a duplicate before you create one.",
    "guideTermCallNumber": "Call number",
    "guideTermCallNumberMeaning": "Where the copy lives on your shelves. It is yours to decide - Dewey, a subject prefix, or a simple shelf code - as long as it is consistent.",
    "guideTermBarcode": "Barcode",
    "guideTermBarcodeMeaning": "The number that identifies one copy to a scanner. Two copies must never share one.",
    "guideTermFormat": "Format",
    "guideTermFormatMeaning": "What kind of thing it is: book, periodical, audio, map. Reports group the collection by it.",
    "guideTermHoldShelf": "Hold shelf",
    "guideTermHoldShelfMeaning": "Where a returned copy waits for the member who reserved it. After the set number of days it goes back into circulation.",
    "guideTermRenewal": "Renewal",
    "guideTermRenewalMeaning": "Extending a loan that is already open. The rules cap how many times, and a title someone is waiting for can be blocked from renewing.",
    "guideTermOverdue": "Overdue",
    "guideTermOverdueMeaning": "Past its due date and not yet returned. Fines are calculated from the day it went overdue, after any grace days.",
    "guideTermMemberType": "Membership category",
    "guideTermMemberTypeMeaning": "A group of members who share limits: how many copies, for how long, and for how many months the membership runs.",
    "guideTermCardNumber": "Card number",
    "guideTermCardNumberMeaning": "The number on the member's card. It is how the desk finds them in one keystroke instead of spelling a name.",
    "guideTermStanding": "Standing",
    "guideTermStandingMeaning": "Whether a member may borrow right now. An expired membership, unreturned overdue copies or unpaid fines can stop them.",
    "@guideTermStanding": {
            "description": "Guide - the shared vocabulary, reused across several articles"
    },

    "guideDashboardTitle": "Dashboard",
    "guideDashboardSummary": "What the opening screen is telling you, and what to do about each number on it.",
    "guideDashboardReadingHeading": "Reading the numbers",
    "guideDashboardReadingBody": "The dashboard answers one question: is anything wrong today? The tiles across the top are counts as of this moment, the list under them is recent desk activity, and the buttons at the bottom are the two things you do most.",
    "guideDashboardShotCaption": "The dashboard, top to bottom",
    "guideDashboardShotMarker1": "Counts for today. A figure that is not zero where you expect zero is the thing to look at first.",
    "guideDashboardShotMarker2": "Recent activity - the last checkouts and returns, newest first. Useful for spotting a mis-scan a minute after it happens.",
    "guideDashboardShotMarker3": "The two desk actions, one click away, so a queue at the counter never waits on navigation.",
    "guideDashboardActingHeading": "Acting on what you see",
    "guideDashboardActingStep1Title": "Overdue is not zero",
    "guideDashboardActingStep1Body": "Open the loan list and sort by due date. The oldest overdue copies are the ones least likely to come back on their own, so chase those first.",
    "guideDashboardActingStep2Title": "A queue at the counter",
    "guideDashboardActingStep2Body": "Go straight to Check out. Find the member by card number, scan every copy, confirm once. Nothing else needs to be open.",
    "guideDashboardActingStep3Title": "Someone asks how the month went",
    "guideDashboardActingStep3Body": "The dashboard is today. Reports is the period - pick the month there and export it rather than counting tiles.",
    "guideDashboardFiguresTitle": "The tiles are live, not saved",
    "guideDashboardFiguresBody": "Every count is recomputed from the catalogue when the screen opens. There is no cached figure to go stale, and no number here that a report will contradict.",
    "@guideDashboardFiguresBody": {
            "description": "Guide - the dashboard article"
    },

    "guideCatalogTitle": "Catalogue",
    "guideCatalogSummary": "Titles, copies, labels and barcodes - how the collection is recorded, and why one book can be two records.",
    "guideCatalogModelHeading": "Titles and copies",
    "guideCatalogModelBody": "This is the one idea that surprises people, and everything else follows from it. A title is a work. A copy is an object. You catalogue the work once and add a copy for every item on the shelf.",
    "guideCatalogOneTitleTitle": "One title, many copies",
    "guideCatalogOneTitleBody": "If you own four of the same book, create the title once and add four copies. Creating it four times gives you four records to correct when the author's name is misspelled, and a report that says you hold four different books.",
    "guideCatalogAddHeading": "Adding to the collection",
    "guideCatalogShotCaption": "The title list",
    "guideCatalogShotMarker1": "Search runs over titles, authors and ISBNs as you type, and the button opens the form for a work you do not have yet.",
    "guideCatalogShotMarker2": "One row per work. The availability column is how many of its copies are on the shelf right now, not how many you own.",
    "guideCatalogAddStep1Title": "Search before you add",
    "guideCatalogAddStep1Body": "Type the ISBN, or the first words of the title. If it is already catalogued, add a copy to it instead of creating a second record for the same work.",
    "guideCatalogAddStep2Title": "Record the work",
    "guideCatalogAddStep2Body": "Author, ISBN, publisher, year, subject and format. Fill what you have - the only thing the app insists on is a title - but an ISBN and a subject are what make the collection searchable a year from now.",
    "guideCatalogAddStep3Title": "Add a copy for each item",
    "guideCatalogAddStep3Body": "Each copy gets its own barcode and call number. Leave the barcode blank and Khulla builds the next one from the pattern in the library profile.",
    "guideCatalogAddStep4Title": "Label the copies",
    "guideCatalogAddStep4Body": "Print a spine label and a barcode sticker so a scanner can read the copy at the desk. A copy without a barcode has to be typed in by hand every time it moves.",
    "guideCatalogLabelsHeading": "Labels and barcodes",
    "guideCatalogLabelsBody": "The label desk prints spine labels and barcode stickers for copies you select, laid out on a sheet. Print for a whole batch of new copies at once rather than one at a time, and keep a scanner at the counter - it turns a thirty-second checkout into a five-second one.",
    "guideCatalogLabelsWarnTitle": "Check the first sheet before you print fifty",
    "guideCatalogLabelsWarnBody": "Printers scale pages differently. Print one sheet, hold a sticker against a book, and confirm a scanner reads it before committing a full sheet of labels to paper.",
    "guideCatalogFaqHeading": "Common questions",
    "guideCatalogFaq1Q": "I added the same book twice. What now?",
    "guideCatalogFaq1A": "Move the copies onto the record you want to keep, then delete the empty duplicate title. Deleting a title that still has copies is refused, which is the app protecting loan history rather than being awkward.",
    "guideCatalogFaq2Q": "A copy is lost or damaged. Do I delete it?",
    "guideCatalogFaq2A": "No. Mark its status instead. Deleting the copy deletes the fact that it was ever borrowed, and next year's inventory will not explain the gap.",
    "guideCatalogFaq3Q": "What do I put in the call number?",
    "guideCatalogFaq3A": "Whatever you shelve by. A small library can use a subject code and a running number. Consistency matters far more than which system you pick.",
    "guideCatalogFaq4Q": "Do I need an ISBN?",
    "guideCatalogFaq4A": "No, and older books and locally published material often have none. Leave it blank rather than inventing one.",
    "@guideCatalogFaq4A": {
            "description": "Guide - the catalogue article"
    },

    "guideCirculationTitle": "Circulation",
    "guideCirculationSummary": "Check out, return, holds and fines - the four things the desk does all day.",
    "guideCirculationCheckOutHeading": "Checking out",
    "guideCirculationCheckOutBody": "A checkout pairs one member with one or more copies and opens a loan for each. The due date is not typed - it comes from the loan rules and the member's category, which is why those are set up first.",
    "guideCirculationShotCaption": "The checkout desk",
    "guideCirculationShotMarker1": "Member first, then copies. The member decides the loan period and the borrowing limit, so the form asks for them in that order.",
    "guideCirculationShotMarker2": "Everything going home with them, listed before you confirm. Check the count against the pile on the counter.",
    "guideCirculationCheckOutStep1Title": "Find the member",
    "guideCirculationCheckOutStep1Body": "Scan or type the card number. Name and phone also work, but the card number is unambiguous and two members can share a name.",
    "guideCirculationCheckOutStep2Title": "Scan every copy",
    "guideCirculationCheckOutStep2Body": "Each barcode adds a row. The summary shows the due date, how many copies this member may still take, and any fines they already owe.",
    "guideCirculationCheckOutStep3Title": "Confirm once",
    "guideCirculationCheckOutStep3Body": "One confirmation writes every loan in the list. If the member is over their limit or blocked, the desk says so here rather than after they have left.",
    "guideCirculationReturnHeading": "Taking copies back",
    "guideCirculationReturnBody": "Scan the copy at the return desk and the loan closes. If it is late, the fine is calculated and recorded at that moment from the rate, the grace days and the number of days overdue - nobody has to work it out, and nobody can forget.",
    "guideCirculationReturnWarnTitle": "Return the copy even when the member cannot pay",
    "guideCirculationReturnWarnBody": "The fine is recorded against the member, not against the copy. Holding the return back until they pay keeps the copy off the shelf and the loan open, which makes every count wrong until it is sorted out.",
    "guideCirculationHoldsHeading": "Holds and renewals",
    "guideCirculationHoldsBody": "When every copy of a title is out, a member can join the queue. The next copy returned is set aside for the first person waiting, and it sits on the hold shelf for the number of days the rules allow before moving on to the next member.",
    "guideCirculationFinesHeading": "Fines",
    "guideCirculationFinesBody": "The fines ledger lists what was raised, what was collected and what was waived. Fines are raised automatically on a late return; collecting or waiving one is a deliberate act by a named member of staff.",
    "guideCirculationFinesStep1Title": "Find the fine",
    "guideCirculationFinesStep1Body": "The ledger lists by member and by date. A member's own record also shows what they owe, which is usually the faster route when they are standing in front of you.",
    "guideCirculationFinesStep2Title": "Record what happened",
    "guideCirculationFinesStep2Body": "Mark it paid when money changes hands, or waived when the library decides to let it go. Both are recorded with the amount.",
    "guideCirculationFinesStep3Title": "Adjust the rate, not the record",
    "guideCirculationFinesStep3Body": "If fines are coming out too high across the board, the rate in the loan rules is wrong. Change it there - editing past fines one at a time hides the actual problem.",
    "guideCirculationWaiveTitle": "A waiver is a permanent record",
    "guideCirculationWaiveBody": "Waiving a fine is written down against the account that did it, and it does not go away. That is the point: on a shared desk machine, it is the only thing that can tell a generous decision from a quiet one.",
    "guideCirculationFaqHeading": "Common questions",
    "guideCirculationFaq1Q": "I checked out the wrong copy. How do I undo it?",
    "guideCirculationFaq1A": "Return it immediately. A loan opened and closed in the same minute raises no fine and leaves an honest trail, which an edited record would not.",
    "guideCirculationFaq2Q": "A member wants to keep a book longer.",
    "guideCirculationFaq2A": "Renew the loan. The rules cap how many renewals are allowed, and a title somebody is waiting for can be held back from renewing so the queue keeps moving.",
    "guideCirculationFaq3Q": "The barcode will not scan.",
    "guideCirculationFaq3A": "Type the number instead - every scan field accepts typing. If a particular sticker never scans, reprint it from the label desk.",
    "guideCirculationFaq4Q": "Can I check out to a blocked member?",
    "guideCirculationFaq4A": "Not while the block stands. Clear what caused it - take the overdue copy back, settle or waive the fine, renew the membership - and the desk lets the loan through.",
    "@guideCirculationFaq4A": {
            "description": "Guide - the circulation article"
    },

    "guideMembersTitle": "Members",
    "guideMembersSummary": "Registering borrowers, the categories that set their limits, and what stops someone borrowing.",
    "guideMembersRegisterHeading": "Registering a borrower",
    "guideMembersRegisterBody": "A member record is short on purpose: enough to identify the person and reach them about a late book, and nothing the library does not need to hold.",
    "guideMembersShotCaption": "The member register",
    "guideMembersShotMarker1": "Search by name, card number or phone. The button opens the registration form.",
    "guideMembersShotMarker2": "A row that reads in a warning colour is a member who cannot borrow right now - expired, blocked, or over the fine limit.",
    "guideMembersRegisterStep1Title": "Check they are not already registered",
    "guideMembersRegisterStep1Body": "Search the phone number first. A member registered twice has their borrowing limit twice over, and their history split between two records.",
    "guideMembersRegisterStep2Title": "Record the person",
    "guideMembersRegisterStep2Body": "Name and a way to reach them. The card number can be left blank and Khulla issues the next one.",
    "guideMembersRegisterStep3Title": "Choose a category",
    "guideMembersRegisterStep3Body": "This is the important field. It sets how many copies they may hold, for how long, and when the membership runs out. Change the category and their limits change with it.",
    "guideMembersCategoriesHeading": "Categories and limits",
    "guideMembersCategoriesBody": "Rather than setting limits per person, set them per category, and put each member in one. A student borrows three for a fortnight, a teacher ten for a term - edit the category once and everyone in it moves together.",
    "guideMembersStandingHeading": "When someone cannot borrow",
    "guideMembersStandingBody": "Three things stop a loan: the membership has expired, the member is holding an overdue copy while the rules block that, or they owe more than the maximum outstanding fine. The member's record says which, so the desk can answer the question without guessing.",
    "guideMembersBlockedTitle": "A block is a rule, not a punishment",
    "guideMembersBlockedBody": "Every block traces back to a setting in the loan rules. If the desk is blocking people you would rather let borrow, change the rule instead of working around it member by member.",
    "guideMembersFaq1Q": "A member lost their card.",
    "guideMembersFaq1A": "Issue a new card number on their existing record. Keep the record itself - it carries their loan history and anything they still owe.",
    "guideMembersFaq2Q": "Can I delete a member who left?",
    "guideMembersFaq2A": "You can, but let the membership expire instead if they have history worth keeping. Deleting is for a record created by mistake.",
    "guideMembersFaq3Q": "Two people have the same name.",
    "guideMembersFaq3A": "That is what card numbers are for. Search by card number at the desk and the ambiguity never comes up.",
    "@guideMembersFaq3A": {
            "description": "Guide - the members article"
    },

    "guideReportsTitle": "Reports",
    "guideReportsSummary": "The questions a committee asks, where each answer lives, and how to get it out as a file.",
    "guideReportsQuestionsHeading": "What each report answers",
    "guideReportsQuestionsBody": "Pick a period at the top and every figure on the screen follows it. The dashboard is today; this is any stretch of time you choose.",
    "guideReportsCirculationMeaning": "How busy the library was - loans issued against copies returned, over the period. This is the number a funding request usually wants.",
    "guideReportsCollectionMeaning": "What you actually hold, broken down by format. Useful for spotting that a collection is ninety per cent one thing.",
    "guideReportsMembersMeaning": "Members joining, month by month. Growth, or the lack of it, after a campaign.",
    "guideReportsFinesMeaning": "Raised, collected and waived. The gap between raised and collected is the money the library decided not to chase.",
    "guideReportsTopTitlesMeaning": "What moves most, ranked. Read it before buying duplicates, and read the bottom of it before buying more of the same.",
    "guideReportsShotCaption": "A report period",
    "guideReportsShotMarker1": "Headline figures for the period you picked, not for today.",
    "guideReportsShotMarker2": "The detail behind them, ranked. Every table on this screen exports as it stands.",
    "guideReportsExportHeading": "Getting the numbers out",
    "guideReportsExportStep1Title": "Set the period first",
    "guideReportsExportStep1Body": "The export carries whatever is on screen, so choose the month or the year before you export rather than trimming the file afterwards.",
    "guideReportsExportStep2Title": "Export the table",
    "guideReportsExportStep2Body": "Each report writes a CSV file, which opens in any spreadsheet. Nothing is sent anywhere - the file lands on this computer.",
    "guideReportsExportStep3Title": "Keep the file with its date",
    "guideReportsExportStep3Body": "A report exported in April and reopened in July shows April, and the figures will not match the app any more. Name the file for its period so nobody quotes it as current.",
    "guideReportsExportTipTitle": "An export is a photograph, not a mirror",
    "guideReportsExportTipBody": "Once written, the file never changes. Re-export rather than editing an old one when the numbers move.",
    "@guideReportsExportTipBody": {
            "description": "Guide - the reports article"
    },

    "guideStaffTitle": "Staff and roles",
    "guideStaffSummary": "Who may do what, why everyone needs their own account, and how to recover a forgotten password.",
    "guideStaffAccountsHeading": "Accounts",
    "guideStaffAccountsBody": "Everyone who works the desk gets an account of their own. It is not about secrecy - it is that a waived fine, a deleted copy and a changed rule are all recorded against whoever did them, and a shared login records all three against nobody.",
    "guideStaffAccountsStep1Title": "Create the account",
    "guideStaffAccountsStep1Body": "Name, email and a first password. Ask the person to change it the first time they sign in.",
    "guideStaffAccountsStep2Title": "Give it the smallest role that works",
    "guideStaffAccountsStep2Body": "Start from the role that covers the job they actually do. Widening a role later takes seconds; explaining a deleted catalogue does not.",
    "guideStaffAccountsStep3Title": "Close the account when they leave",
    "guideStaffAccountsStep3Body": "Deactivate rather than delete. The account stops working immediately, and everything it did stays attributed.",
    "guideStaffRolesHeading": "The four roles",
    "guideStaffRolesBody": "A role is a set of permissions, and each permission has two levels: seeing a section, and changing what is in it. Read-only sees without changing.",
    "guideStaffRoleAdminMeaning": "Everything, including staff accounts, settings and backups. Keep the number of administrators small - one or two.",
    "guideStaffRoleLibrarianMeaning": "The whole library: catalogue, circulation, members, fines and reports. Not staff accounts or the destructive settings.",
    "guideStaffRoleAssistantMeaning": "The desk. Check out, return, and look things up. Suits a volunteer or a part-time helper who should not be reshaping the catalogue.",
    "guideStaffRoleReadOnlyMeaning": "Looks, changes nothing. For a reading-room terminal or someone auditing the collection.",
    "guideStaffSharedTitle": "Do not share one login",
    "guideStaffSharedBody": "One shared account makes every record in the app anonymous. When a copy goes missing or a fine is waived, the only honest answer the app can give is the name it was told - and a shared login means it was told nothing.",
    "guideStaffPasswordsHeading": "Passwords and recovery",
    "guideStaffPasswordsBody": "An administrator can reset any other account's password from the staff list. The first administrator is the exception: there is nobody above them, so setup issues a one-time recovery code. Print it and keep it somewhere other than this computer.",
    "guideStaffFaq1Q": "We lost the administrator password and the recovery code.",
    "guideStaffFaq1A": "There is no back door, by design - one would let anyone with the file read the library's records. Another administrator account can reset the password; if there is none, the catalogue file can be opened only by restoring it into a fresh installation.",
    "guideStaffFaq2Q": "Can I change what a role is allowed to do?",
    "guideStaffFaq2A": "The four roles are fixed, so that what a role means is the same in every Khulla library. Move the person to a different role instead.",
    "guideStaffFaq3Q": "Someone forgot to sign out on the shared machine.",
    "guideStaffFaq3A": "Sign out from the account menu at the foot of the rail before handing the desk over. It costs nothing - the catalogue is untouched and the next person signs straight in.",
    "@guideStaffFaq3A": {
            "description": "Guide - the staff and roles article"
    },

    "guideSettingsTitle": "Settings and backup",
    "guideSettingsSummary": "The library's own details, the rules every loan follows, and the export that protects all of it.",
    "guideSettingsProfileHeading": "The library profile",
    "guideSettingsProfileBody": "Name, contact details and currency. These are not decoration: the contact line is printed on overdue notices so a member knows who to call, and the currency is what every fine and fee in the app is shown in.",
    "guideSettingsShotCaption": "The settings sections",
    "guideSettingsShotMarker1": "Four groups. Profile and rules are the library's record; appearance is this device only.",
    "guideSettingsShotMarker2": "Each group is a form with its own save. Nothing is written until you save it.",
    "guideSettingsRulesHeading": "Loan rules",
    "guideSettingsRulesBody": "This screen decides what every future loan does. Each field has a direct effect at the desk.",
    "guideSettingsRuleLoanPeriod": "How many days a copy goes out for. A member's category can override it.",
    "guideSettingsRuleRenewals": "How many times one loan may be extended before the copy has to come back.",
    "guideSettingsRuleBorrowLimit": "How many copies one member may hold at once. The checkout desk refuses the one that goes over.",
    "guideSettingsRuleFinePerDay": "What one late day costs. Multiplied by the days overdue, after the grace days.",
    "guideSettingsRuleGraceDays": "Days of lateness that cost nothing. Set this to two or three unless your library is strict - it saves a great many small arguments.",
    "guideSettingsRuleMaxOutstanding": "How much a member may owe before the desk stops lending to them.",
    "guideSettingsRulesForwardTitle": "Rules apply to new loans, not old ones",
    "guideSettingsRulesForwardBody": "Changing the loan period does not move the due date of a book already out. Loans keep the terms they were issued under, which is what makes a due date something you can stand behind.",
    "guideSettingsAppearanceHeading": "Appearance and language",
    "guideSettingsAppearanceBody": "Theme, accent colour and language are this device's preference, stored on this computer rather than in the catalogue. Change them freely: on a shared machine each installation keeps its own, and nothing here reaches the library's records.",
    "guideSettingsBackupHeading": "Backup and restore",
    "guideSettingsBackupBody": "This is the most important screen in the app. The catalogue is a file on this computer, and a computer can be stolen, dropped or simply fail. An export is a complete copy of the library in one file.",
    "guideSettingsBackupStep1Title": "Export the catalogue",
    "guideSettingsBackupStep1Body": "One file holds every title, copy, member, loan and fine. It takes seconds even for a large library.",
    "guideSettingsBackupStep2Title": "Put it somewhere else",
    "guideSettingsBackupStep2Body": "A pen drive, another computer, a cloud drive. A backup sitting on the same machine as the catalogue protects you from nothing at all.",
    "guideSettingsBackupStep3Title": "Do it on a schedule you will keep",
    "guideSettingsBackupStep3Body": "Every closing time is ideal. Every Friday is realistic. Whatever you choose, the worst case is losing the work since the last one, so choose an interval you can afford to lose.",
    "guideSettingsRestoreTitle": "Restoring replaces everything",
    "guideSettingsRestoreBody": "A restore is not a merge. It overwrites the catalogue on this computer with the contents of the file, and anything entered since that file was written is gone. Export the current catalogue first, even if you believe it is worthless.",
    "guideSettingsFaq1Q": "Where is my catalogue file?",
    "guideSettingsFaq1A": "In this computer's application data folder, not in Documents. Copy the exported backup rather than hunting for the live file - the app may have it open.",
    "guideSettingsFaq2Q": "Can I move the library to a new computer?",
    "guideSettingsFaq2A": "Yes. Install Khulla there, export from the old machine, and restore into the new one. Check a few records afterwards before you retire the old machine.",
    "guideSettingsFaq3Q": "How big does the file get?",
    "guideSettingsFaq3A": "Small. A library of tens of thousands of records is still a file you can email, because it holds text rather than images.",
    "@guideSettingsFaq3A": {
            "description": "Guide - the settings and backup article"
    }
}
